latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Divide the 11x9 input grid into four quadrants (top-left, top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right), ignoring the central yellow line.\n   b. For each quadrant, count the occurrences of each color, excluding black (0) and yellow (4).\n   c. Calculate the total number of colored cells (non-black, non-yellow) in each quadrant.\n\n2. Determine color representation:\n   a. For each quadrant, calculate the percentage of each color's occurrence.\n   b. Select the top three colors in each quadrant that have at least 15% representation.\n   c. If fewer than three colors meet this criterion, include colors with lower percentages until you have three colors or run out of colors.\n\n3. Create a color palette:\n   a. Combine the selected colors from all quadrants into a single list.\n   b. Remove duplicates to get a set of unique colors to be used in the output.\n\n4. Initialize the 5x4 output grid:\n   a. Create an empty 5x4 grid.\n   b. Assign each 2x2 corner of the output grid to correspond with a quadrant of the input grid.\n\n5. Fill the corner 2x2 areas:\n   a. For each 2x2 corner, use the top two colors from its corresponding input quadrant.\n   b. Place the most dominant color in the corner cell closest to its original position in the input.\n   c. Place the second most dominant color in the diagonally opposite cell.\n   d. Fill the remaining two cells randomly with these two colors.\n\n6. Fill the central column (3rd column):\n   a. For each row in the central column, randomly select a color from the palette that appears in either adjacent cell in that row.\n   b. Ensure that no color appears more than twice in this column.\n\n7. Balance color distribution:\n   a. Count the occurrences of each color in the output grid.\n   b. If any color appears more than 5 times, replace excess occurrences with less represented colors from the palette.\n   c. If any color from the palette is not represented in the output, replace a random occurrence of the most common color with this missing color.\n\n8. Handle black cells:\n   a. Calculate the percentage of black cells in each input quadrant.\n   b. If a quadrant has more than 25% black cells, ensure at least one black cell in the corresponding 2x2 area of the output.\n   c. Replace a randomly chosen color cell with black, preferring to replace less dominant colors.\n\n9. Final adjustment:\n   a. Ensure that the top-left and bottom-right cells of the output grid match the most dominant non-black colors of the corresponding input quadrants.\n   b. If this isn't already the case, swap the current colors in these positions with the required colors, maintaining overall color balance.\n\n10. Create and return the ColoredGrid:\n    a. Use the filled 5x4 grid to create a new ColoredGrid object.\n    b. Return this ColoredGrid as the solution.\n\nThis plan aims to capture the essence of the input grid's color distribution and spatial relationships while allowing for the necessary abstraction in the smaller output grid. It provides a systematic approach to color selection and placement, with mechanisms to ensure color diversity and maintain key spatial characteristics. The plan is designed to be flexible enough to handle various input patterns while producing consistent and representative output grids."
